# Shell scripts run on Linux (the server, deploy tooling) — they MUST stay LF, or a
# checkout on a CRLF platform corrupts the shebang (`bad interpreter: bash^M`).
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
# cron reads these on Linux; a CRLF checkout makes the command line end in .
*.cron  text eol=lf

# DO NOT add an eol rule for migrations/*.sql.
#
# sqlx checksums migration BYTES at compile time and compares them against the
# _sqlx_migrations table at startup, so the on-disk encoding is load-bearing
# FOREVER for every migration already applied in production. Production's
# recorded checksums were computed from CRLF bytes (verified 2026-07-27:
# _sqlx_migrations.checksum for version 1 == sha384 of the CRLF file).
#
# Adding `migrations/*.sql text eol=lf` here looks like a fix for the
# local-worktree mismatch, but `git archive` applies gitattributes — so the
# deploy tarball silently became LF, every checksum changed, and the backend
# crash-looped on VersionMismatch(1) until rollback. Left unset, the files
# travel as committed and keep matching prod.
#
# If these ever need renormalising, prod's _sqlx_migrations checksums must be
# rewritten in the same controlled step — it is a data migration, not a
# whitespace change.
#
# PRACTICAL RULE (learned the hard way THREE times, last on 2026-08-03):
# prod's recorded byte history is MIXED — migrations 1-13 were applied from
# CRLF bytes, 14+ from LF — so no rule of thumb about platform or line endings
# is safe. The long-lived main checkout preserves that exact fossil record; a
# FRESH checkout (clone OR `git worktree add`) re-materialises uniform CRLF
# via `* text=auto` and mismatches every LF-recorded migration: shipping a
# tarball from a fresh worktree crash-looped the primary on VersionMismatch(14)
# until a binary rollback. The tarball must byte-match every host's
# _sqlx_migrations checksums — dual-ship.sh backend now VERIFIES this against
# each host's database before building and refuses to ship on any mismatch,
# so the invariant is a hard gate rather than this comment.
#
# Symptom of the same mismatch locally: a dev DB that applied a migration
# BEFORE git first normalised the file (e.g. a brand-new migration written,
# run, and only then committed) records the LF checksum and then refuses to
# start once the file is checked out as CRLF. Fix is dev-only —
# `DELETE FROM _sqlx_migrations WHERE version = <n>;` and let it re-apply,
# which is safe because every migration here is written idempotently.


# Let git pick line endings normally for everything else.
*       text=auto

# Applied migrations are FROZEN BYTES, not text. `-text` disables normalisation
# in both directions: git stores and checks out these files verbatim, so a fresh
# clone on any platform reproduces production's recorded bytes exactly.
#
# This replaces relying on "do not add an eol rule" above, which protected the
# long-lived checkout but NOT a fresh one — and a fresh clone is now the normal
# way to get this repo. Measured before adding this: the long-lived checkout
# matched all 51 recorded checksums; a fresh clone matched 20 and would have
# crash-looped the backend on VersionMismatch.
#
# Content here is equally frozen. A rebrand pass rewrote comments in four
# applied migrations and a trigger-function name in a fifth, which changes their
# sha384 and blocks startup just as surely as a line ending does. Applied
# migrations are an append-only ledger: to change one you rewrite production's
# _sqlx_migrations rows in the same controlled step, which is a data migration,
# not a rename. That is why "Sovereign" still appears in some of these files.
migrations/*.sql -text
